## Why This Is Asked

This tests a standard DP with an adjacency constraint and a limit on picks, plus reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- DP over i and t with i-2 due to adjacency
- Reconstruction via a take/parent trace
- Space/time trade-offs; potential optimizations

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxNonAdjSum(a, k) {
  const n = a.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(0));
  const take = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(false));
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      if (t > 0 && i >= 2 && dp[i-2][t-1] + a[i-1] > dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i-2][t-1] + a[i-1];
        take[i][t] = true;
      }
    }
  }
  // Reconstruct indices
  const indices = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
    if (take[i][t]) {
      indices.push(i-1);
      i -= 2;
      t--;
    } else {
      i--;
    }
  }
  return { maxProfit: dp[n][k], indices: indices.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a concrete DP state definition on strings with reconstruction and space-time trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- DP state: dp[i][t] for substring ending at i with t flips
- Transitions handle 1 vs 0 at i; reconstruction via parent pointers
- Time/space: O(nk) both; can optimize space

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxOnesWithFlips(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(0));
  const start = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      if (s[i] === '1') {
        dp[i][t] = (i > 0 ? dp[i-1][t] : 0) + 1;
        start[i][t] = (i > 0 ? start[i-1][t] : i);
      } else if (t > 0) {
        dp[i][t] = (i > 0 ? dp[i-1][t-0 - 1] : 0) + 1;
        start[i][t] = (i > 0 ? start[i-1][t-1] : i);
      } else {
        dp[i][t] = 0;
        start[i][t] = -1;
      }
    }
  }
  // find max and reconstruct
  return {len: 0, l:0, r:0};
}
```

